[Music]  hey everyone welcome to another  installment of harry potter theory as  you may remember or preferably may have  watched a few weeks back i did a video  on the major differences between the  book and film versions of hermione  granger well some of you requested that  i also take a look at the biggest  differences between ron and harry as  well  so today we'll be discussing the most  noticeable differences between ron  weasley when comparing the novels to the  films what about harry keep an eye out  for a video about the differences  between his character as i'll certainly  be releasing that in the near future  but for now let's begin with ron shall  we ah ron weasley if you know him best  from his character depiction in the  harry potter book series then the ronald  weasley that you know and love is the  courageous quick-witted and loyal best  mate of harry potter he's also the  youngest son and sixth child of molly  and arthur weasley  and while some of that remains true for  ron's character in the films the  majority of what's consistent between  the books and the movies is rather  surface level i mean yes ron is one of  harry's bestest buds in the movies  and yes he remains the sixth son born to  mr and mrs weasley  but other than the superficial details  there are many more character defining  qualities that were either changed  substantially for the films or omitted  completely  in this video i'll be focusing on four  of these major differences between book  and film ron major difference number one  his amount of courage and bravery unlike  those who have only read the harry  potter series i dare say very few fans  of the films would describe ron as  courageous  in fact in the movies ron is quite  frequently portrayed as well the  opposite  for example in novel version of the  prisoner of azkaban there's a scene in  which harry ron and hermione come face  to face with sirius black who they  believe to be an evil murderous wizard  who betrayed harry's parents  of course this turns out to be quite  untrue  but before they uncover this important  detail  ron not only places himself between  harry and perceived danger he does so on  a broken leg declaring that he's willing  to die to protect his best friend  in the movie version however he's the  bloke cowering in the corner  while hermione takes what should have  been ron's place shielding harry from  sirius black  sadly this sort of thing happens  frequently throughout the films where  ron's moments of bravery and loyalty are  turned into displays of him being a  complete coward and someone else  typically hermione is given his lines  and actions  another example is in the philosopher's  stone where book ron remains calm after  the three friends get trapped in the  devil's snare  his level-headedness allows him to give  hermione a good reminder that she's a  witch and can summon the thing she needs  which happens to be fire to cast the  spell to help them escape  since hermione is a little hysterical at  this point lamenting they don't have  anything to start a fire ron is  essential to the three friends escaping  from the snare in the film version  however hermione does it all and ron  just sits there screaming  another memorable example is in the  chamber of secrets where ron is able to  confront his fear of spiders he's scared  of course but he manages to summon the  courage to do what he needs to do  but in the film he's a laughable mess  to me this lack of ron's bravery is such  a flaw in the harry potter movie  franchise for several reasons  for one it makes ron seem a lot less  dynamic as a person and almost  unbelievable as harry's best mate  for another in the books it's pretty  clear that ron's courage is one of his  most admirable qualities and in my  opinion the reason the sorting hat  placed him in gryffindor  without his bravery he actually seems  rather out of place in the films amongst  harry and hermione and within gryffindor  as a whole  major difference number two his level of  intelligence  in addition to novel ron's bravery the  films also seem to drastically reduce  movie ron's intelligence in the books  ron and harry frequently receive similar  grades and are able to perform magic at  a pretty comparable level  it's hermione who's well about the both  of them when it comes to academics and  book learning  in the movies however harry's character  is portrayed as being smarter and more  adept at magic than he is in the books  while ron is showcased as well kind of  an idiot  don't tell me you didn't notice  in the philosopher's stone for example  there are several times in the novel  that ron does something right like take  out the troll in the bathroom  in the movies he requires much help from  the others to even do that  he also somehow manages to hit himself  in the face with a broom during a flying  lesson in the first film  despite having grown up in a wizarding  household which presumably had this  magical item and while i'm on that topic  might i also just say that the movies  also took away almost every instance of  ron being the trio's wizarding world  expert when he got to share his  knowledge of magic with harry and  hermione who had both grown up in mughal  homes  for some reason all of ron's helpful  tips and explanations about magic from  the novels are handed over to hermione  who in the films frequently shares  information about things she reasonably  would have had no understanding  or knowledge of  finally in the books ron is actually a  pretty funny and quick witted character  cracking jokes and providing wonderful  comic relief  and while yes you may find movie ron  funny i'd be willing to guess that you  find him humorous because of all the  stupid things he does or incredibly  silly expressions he makes the fact is  that while he's technically comic relief  in both depictions of his character the  films took away ron's humor and quick  wit  replacing it with an over-exaggerated  goofiness that you could laugh at rather  than with  major difference number three his  relationship with harry for some reason  the movies decided to portray hermione  as being closer to harry than ron  and while all three are close friends in  the books it's incredibly clear that  harry and ron are best mates  sorry hermione this alteration from  books to movies is actually kind of  infuriating there are so many scenes in  the book series in which ron was  showcased as caring the most about harry  but in the movies ron is almost always  swapped out with hermione as mentioned  she's the one who throws herself in  front of harry in the prison of azkaban  rather than ron  she's the one who says dumbledore asked  her and ron not to send harry letters  not ron  she's the one who insists she'll go with  harry to hunt horcruxes as a useless and  uncaring version of ron sits silently in  the background major difference than  before his appearance  the translation of a character from the  page to the screen is a hard one for  filmmakers  as discussed in my video on the  differences between book and film  hermione it's of course near impossible  to cast the perfect representation of a  character from a book that everyone will  be satisfied with  especially with a franchise as well  known and well loved as harry potter  however since this is a video on the  differences between book and movie ron  how could i not mention the differences  in his appearance  so when it comes to how ron looked in  the books he was described as tall long  and lanky with bright red hair and  freckles now i would say that the movie  version of ron portrayed by actor rupert  grint could only be described as two of  those things  tall sort of with bright red hair he was  not particularly long and lanky and he  certainly didn't have freckles it's  interesting because the statures of  james and oliver phelps the twins who  played fred and george weasley are more  along the lines of how i envisioned ron  in the books  and rupert grint actually looks a lot  more like how fred and george are  described in the novels in any case this  would have been hard to predict when  casting young children  i guess the one thing the filmmakers  could have done to make rupert grint  better match the description of ron in  the novels is put freckles on him but  when compared to all the other major  character alterations they made to ron  this hardly seems important  does it and that brings us to the end of  another video what did you think do you  agree did the movie turn ron into a  useless version of the ron depicted in  the books  let me know your thoughts in the  comments below  and as always if you enjoyed this video  don't forget to like it and subscribe to  the channel  until next time  akiobrain  you

